WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.720
All right, friends.

00:04.720 --> 00:08.880
So now we come to the part where it is a little bit complicated about the subqueries.

00:08.920 --> 00:13.720
Now we're going to talk about the dependencies between the subquery and the main query.

00:13.720 --> 00:20.240
So far, all the examples and the subqueries that we have learned were a non correlated subquery.

00:20.280 --> 00:26.800
In non correlated subquery means a subquery that can run independently from the main query.

00:26.800 --> 00:30.000
So that means the subquery is like a standalone query.

00:30.000 --> 00:33.920
But in the other hand we have the exact opposite type of the subquery.

00:33.920 --> 00:36.520
We have the correlated subquery.

00:36.560 --> 00:44.920
A correlated subquery is a subquery that relies on values from the main query for each row it processes.

00:44.920 --> 00:49.800
So this means the subquery here is completely dependent on the main query.

00:49.960 --> 00:52.440
So I know this might be a little bit confusing.

00:52.440 --> 00:58.320
That's why we can have the following very simple sketch in order to exactly understand how this works.

00:58.320 --> 01:00.590
So as usual we have a database tables.

01:00.630 --> 01:05.750
And now this time SQL is going to go and start executing the main query first.

01:05.790 --> 01:07.510
This is the first thing happens.

01:07.510 --> 01:13.150
So the main query can go and query the database in order to get results.

01:13.150 --> 01:16.590
And SQL is going to process the results row by row.

01:16.590 --> 01:17.750
So now what can happen.

01:17.750 --> 01:23.030
The main query is going to go and pass the first row informations to the subquery.

01:23.030 --> 01:26.110
So now the subquery is going to get the data from the main query.

01:26.110 --> 01:28.470
So SQL can execute the subquery.

01:28.470 --> 01:32.670
So here the subquery can return a value like for example one.

01:32.670 --> 01:37.830
So here it's very important to understand that now the SQL or the main query is going to check.

01:37.870 --> 01:40.550
Is there a result from the subquery.

01:40.550 --> 01:41.430
And this example.

01:41.430 --> 01:42.710
Yes we have our results.

01:42.710 --> 01:47.950
So here SQL is checking the output for the subquery for the first row.

01:47.950 --> 01:53.710
So if there is a result SQL is going to go and return the row in the final results.

01:53.710 --> 01:57.750
So this is the whole iteration happened only for the first row.

01:57.750 --> 02:02.210
So we're going to process the whole thing again from the start for the second row.

02:02.250 --> 02:05.330
So the main query is going to get the second row from the database.

02:05.330 --> 02:08.090
And it's going to pass it to the subquery.

02:08.130 --> 02:14.730
Once the subquery gets this new information SQL can go and execute the subquery once again.

02:14.810 --> 02:19.490
So now let's say that after executing the subquery there were no results.

02:19.490 --> 02:23.690
So the subquery is not returning anything after the execution.

02:23.730 --> 02:25.090
So now what can happen.

02:25.130 --> 02:27.290
SQL and the main query are going to check okay.

02:27.330 --> 02:30.250
There is no results from the subquery.

02:30.250 --> 02:35.090
And this means this row should be excluded and not presented in the output.

02:35.130 --> 02:37.610
So we will not see this row at the output.

02:37.650 --> 02:43.450
So as you can see SQL is executing the subquery once again for the second row.

02:43.530 --> 02:46.650
So this will keep happening as long as we have row.

02:46.690 --> 02:48.250
For example we have another row.

02:48.290 --> 02:50.450
The main query is going to pass it to the subquery.

02:50.450 --> 02:53.370
The subquery is going to be executed for the third time.

02:53.370 --> 02:55.810
And the result of the subquery is going to be one.

02:55.810 --> 02:58.760
So the same thing can happen is SQL can check it okay.

02:58.760 --> 02:59.680
We have a value.

02:59.680 --> 03:03.920
So this row is allowed to be in the final results and so on.

03:03.960 --> 03:09.760
The cycle can keep repeating for each row that can be retrieved from the main query.

03:09.760 --> 03:14.840
And once we have processed all the rows the final result can be presented in the output.

03:14.840 --> 03:21.440
So what we have understood so far, the correlated subqueries is always depending on the main query,

03:21.440 --> 03:27.440
and the subquery can be executed for each row that we're going to get from the main query.

03:27.440 --> 03:32.520
So in this example we have four rows and the subquery is executed four times.

03:32.520 --> 03:35.000
So this is how the correlated subquery works.

03:35.000 --> 03:39.560
It's a little bit more complicated than the non correlated subquery.

03:39.560 --> 03:42.480
The non correlated subqueries are really straightforward.

03:42.480 --> 03:47.040
So first the subquery going to go and execute the database only once.

03:47.040 --> 03:52.600
And the output of the subquery is going to be like an intermediate result that can be used from the

03:52.600 --> 03:53.640
main query.

03:53.640 --> 03:57.390
So the main query is going to go and query the intermediate results.

03:57.390 --> 04:00.430
And in the output we're going to get the final results.

04:00.430 --> 04:04.750
So as you can see in the execution of the non-correlated subquery it is straightforward.

04:04.790 --> 04:06.150
There is no iterations.

04:06.150 --> 04:08.550
Everything going to be executed only once.

04:08.550 --> 04:14.910
So now if you compare them side by side you can see that with the Non-correlated subquery it is completely

04:14.910 --> 04:17.150
independent from the main query.

04:17.150 --> 04:20.630
So that means the subquery going to be executed only once.

04:20.630 --> 04:26.310
And after that SQL is going to go and it will execute the main query only once using the results from

04:26.310 --> 04:27.270
the subquery.

04:27.270 --> 04:31.470
But on the left side the subquery is going to be executed multiple times.

04:31.470 --> 04:34.510
And it is completely depending on the main query.

04:34.510 --> 04:40.310
And there is like an iteration for each row that's going to be retrieved from the main query.

04:40.310 --> 04:44.510
So the process is going to be cycling until all the rows are processed.

04:44.510 --> 04:49.870
And this is exactly how the correlated and the non-correlated subqueries work in SQL.

04:49.910 --> 04:50.190
All right.

04:50.230 --> 04:51.430
So now let's have the following task.

04:51.430 --> 04:56.860
And it says show all customer details and find the total orders of each customer.

04:56.860 --> 05:00.860
We have already solved this task and you know, in SQL we don't have only one query.

05:00.900 --> 05:04.780
In order to solve something, we have multiple ways in order to do it.

05:04.780 --> 05:08.540
So we solve this task before using the subqueries and the joins.

05:08.580 --> 05:14.660
Now we're going to go and solve this task using subquery in the Select clause and as well using the

05:14.660 --> 05:16.380
correlated subqueries.

05:16.380 --> 05:18.500
So again let's do it step by step.

05:18.540 --> 05:19.420
It's very simple.

05:19.460 --> 05:21.700
First we need all the customer details.

05:22.140 --> 05:27.300
So as we learned select star from sales customers.

05:27.300 --> 05:30.740
So if you execute it you will get all the details of all customers.

05:30.780 --> 05:35.100
Now we need to find the total number of orders of each customer.

05:35.140 --> 05:40.500
Now before we have solved this, using a simple query where we have used the count function together

05:40.500 --> 05:41.380
with a groupby.

05:41.420 --> 05:43.780
But this time we're going to do it a little bit different.

05:43.780 --> 05:53.600
So let's go and write a query saying select count star from the table Sales orders.

05:53.760 --> 05:55.840
So now let's go and execute it.

05:55.880 --> 05:58.360
With that we have the total number of orders.

05:58.360 --> 06:02.840
So let's go and take this subquery and use it in the select.

06:02.840 --> 06:06.200
So we are using it as a scalar subquery.

06:06.200 --> 06:07.920
So let's just put it over here.

06:07.960 --> 06:10.920
And this is the main query.

06:10.960 --> 06:15.040
And in order to make this as a subquery what we're going to do we can have the parentheses.

06:15.040 --> 06:17.240
And we're going to say that total sales.

06:17.480 --> 06:19.200
So now let's go and execute it.

06:19.240 --> 06:22.400
So now as you can see we have here all the details about the customers.

06:22.400 --> 06:23.640
And we have the total sales.

06:23.640 --> 06:26.480
But we have one issue we don't need just the total order.

06:26.480 --> 06:29.080
We need the total orders for each customer.

06:29.080 --> 06:32.000
So each customer has different total orders.

06:32.000 --> 06:34.200
So we cannot have like the following setup.

06:34.200 --> 06:37.080
We cannot say group by customer ID.

06:37.440 --> 06:41.560
And then you have like here the customer ID and so on.

06:41.560 --> 06:44.280
So if you go and execute it you will get a problem.

06:44.280 --> 06:50.000
And that's because if you go and execute this subquery over here you will get like multiple rows and

06:50.000 --> 06:50.990
multiple columns.

06:50.990 --> 06:52.790
So you have like a table query.

06:52.790 --> 06:56.750
And this type of subquery is not allowed to be used in the Select clause.

06:56.750 --> 06:57.190
Right.

06:57.190 --> 06:59.910
We have to have only scalar subquery.

07:00.710 --> 07:02.510
So that's why we cannot do that.

07:02.510 --> 07:04.790
So we have to go and remove all those stuff.

07:05.910 --> 07:10.150
But we can go and solve it using the correlated subqueries.

07:10.150 --> 07:14.590
So now the subquery is completely independent from the main query.

07:14.830 --> 07:18.270
So in order to correlate it what we're going to do we're going to go and connect it.

07:18.270 --> 07:20.510
So I'm going to give aliases for the tables.

07:20.990 --> 07:28.950
And I'm going to say where the customer ID is equal to the customer ID from the main query from the

07:28.950 --> 07:29.750
customers.

07:29.750 --> 07:36.670
So again we are connecting the customer ID from the orders in the subquery with the customer ID from

07:36.670 --> 07:39.670
the table customers that comes from the main query.

07:39.670 --> 07:45.910
So now we are saying okay, execute this only for specific customer not for the whole table.

07:45.910 --> 07:48.350
So let's go and execute it.

07:48.350 --> 07:51.940
So now in the output, we have the total sales for each customer.

07:51.940 --> 07:55.540
And we don't have here like the total sales in the whole table orders.

07:55.540 --> 08:00.180
And that's because what is happening for each row the subquery is going to be executed.

08:00.180 --> 08:04.860
So for the customer number one this query is going to be executed like this.

08:04.900 --> 08:10.140
Count the total number of orders where the customer ID equal to the one.

08:10.140 --> 08:11.860
So let me just show you what this means.

08:11.860 --> 08:15.860
If I go and remove this from here and just put the number one.

08:16.060 --> 08:23.900
So if I go and execute this you will see the customer ID one has three orders and it just put it back

08:23.940 --> 08:24.860
and executes.

08:24.860 --> 08:27.420
And the same thing can happen for each customer.

08:27.420 --> 08:31.260
So for each customer for each row this subquery can be executed.

08:31.260 --> 08:35.380
And it can be filtered with the customer ID that comes from the main query.

08:35.380 --> 08:40.260
So this is another way on how to solve this task using the correlated subqueries.

08:40.260 --> 08:44.460
So now let's summarize and understand quickly what are the differences between the non-correlated and

08:44.460 --> 08:45.940
the correlated subqueries.

08:45.940 --> 08:51.210
So now if you are talking about the definition than non-correlated subquery are subqueries that are

08:51.210 --> 08:53.490
independent of the main query.

08:53.490 --> 08:58.490
But in the other hand, the correlated subqueries are independent of the main query.

08:58.530 --> 09:03.010
And now if you are talking about the execution, the Non-correlated subquery is going to be executed

09:03.050 --> 09:04.130
only once.

09:04.170 --> 09:06.810
And then the result is going to be used by the main query.

09:06.810 --> 09:12.610
But by the correlated subqueries, the subquery is going to be executed for each row that we have from

09:12.610 --> 09:13.610
the main query.

09:13.610 --> 09:18.410
And as we learned for the Non-correlated subqueries, we can execute it on its own.

09:18.410 --> 09:20.490
So we can go and select it and execute it.

09:20.490 --> 09:24.410
But the correlated subqueries, we cannot execute it on its own.

09:24.410 --> 09:26.810
So we have to execute always the whole thing.

09:26.850 --> 09:32.050
And if you are talking about which one is easier, I think it's clear that the non-correlated subqueries

09:32.050 --> 09:33.970
are easier to write and to read.

09:33.970 --> 09:37.410
And in the other hand, the correlated subqueries are harder to read.

09:37.570 --> 09:38.970
And as well it's complex.

09:39.010 --> 09:41.850
Now we are talking about the performance of the database.

09:41.890 --> 09:45.010
Since the correlated subquery is going to be executed only once.

09:45.050 --> 09:49.510
This of course is going to lead to have a better performance because things are really straightforward

09:49.510 --> 09:50.670
and not complicated.

09:50.670 --> 09:55.830
But in the other hand, with the correlated subqueries, there is more efforts because SQL has to check

09:55.870 --> 09:59.230
a lot of stuff and the subquery is going to be executed many times.

09:59.230 --> 10:01.590
So the non-correlated subqueries are faster.

10:01.630 --> 10:06.550
We use the Non-correlated subqueries in order to do static comparison.

10:06.550 --> 10:10.430
So the value that we are getting from the subquery is executed only once.

10:10.430 --> 10:14.830
And we will get only one static value in order to use it for filtering and so on.

10:14.830 --> 10:19.470
But in the other hand we use correlated subqueries in order to do row by row comparison.

10:19.470 --> 10:23.310
And since we don't have here a static value, each time the subquery is going to run, we're going to

10:23.310 --> 10:24.390
have different results.

10:24.390 --> 10:26.590
This can add more dynamic to the filters.

10:26.590 --> 10:28.310
And we don't have a static value.

10:28.310 --> 10:32.790
So those are the big differences between the non-correlated and the correlated subqueries.

10:32.830 --> 10:33.230
All right.

10:33.230 --> 10:38.990
So now after we understood the concept of the two types correlated and non-correlated subqueries, we're

10:38.990 --> 10:44.270
going to go now and cover the last operator for the subqueries we have the exists.

10:44.270 --> 10:45.910
So what is exist operator.
